{"name":"Forsythia Bud","hex":"#bbcc55","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"forsythia","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several shrubs, of the genus Forsythia, native to Asia and Eastern Europe, that are cultivated for their yellow flowers, which bloom in early spring."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/forsythia"},{"term":"bud","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Ntcham."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A newly sprouted leaf or blossom that has not yet unfolded.","Something that has begun to develop."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bud"}],"entries":[{"title":"Forsythia","blocks":[{"text":"Forsythia, is a genus of flowering plants in the olive family Oleaceae. There are about 11 species, mostly native to Eastern Asia, but one native to Southeastern Europe. Forsythia – also one of the plant's common names – is named after the botanist William Forsyth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Forsythia_flower.JPG/330px-Forsythia_flower.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forsythia","wikidata":"Q672980"},{"title":"Bud","blocks":[{"text":"In botany, a bud is an undeveloped or embryonic shoot and normally occurs in the axil of a leaf or at the tip of a stem. Once formed, a bud may remain for some time in a dormant condition, or it may form a shoot immediately. Buds may be specialized to develop flowers or short shoots or may have the potential for general shoot development. The term bud is also used in zoology, where it refers to an outgrowth from the body which can develop into a new individual."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Fagus_sylvatica_bud.jpg/330px-Fagus_sylvatica_bud.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bud","wikidata":"Q189838"}],"images